  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1125 AM MST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nFor Tuesday night and Wednesday, a weak backdoor front is \nexpected to push into eastern New Mexico. This will bring a \ntemporary shift to breezy north/northeasterly winds and a slight \ncooling trend for the eastern plains. Elsewhere, upper-level \nridging maintains unseasonably mild temperatures. By Wednesday \nafternoon, temperatures across central and western NM are \nprojected to reach the low to mid-60s, while eastern areas will \nlikely see highs slightly cooler behind the front, generally in \nthe upper 50s to low 60s. Model consensus is high on the lack of \nsynoptic scale forcing through Wednesday, keeping PoPs at near \nzero.\n\nThe quiet weather pattern remains firmly in place through Friday \nas the upper-level ridge slowly migrates eastward. The National \nBlend of Models (NBM) maintains high confidence in dry conditions,\nwith highs consistently reaching near or a little above mid- \nNovember averages.\n\nBy Saturday, the synoptic pattern begins to shift as the longwave\ntrough over the Pacific Northwest deepens and a southern stream\nshortwave moves into Baja California. While deterministic models\ninitially disagreed on the track of this system, there is now\nbetter agreement in moving a trough across the Southwest by\nSaturday night and Sunday. The NBM and ensemble guidance suggest\nan increase in moisture, with PoPs rising to 20-30% across the\nwest and south by Sunday. Temperatures will begin a slight\ndownward trend Saturday and Sunday as cloud cover increases and\nthe airmass modifies ahead of the approaching trough. While\nsignificant impacts are not currently expected, mountain snow and\nlight valley rain showers will be possible as the system tracks\nacross the region.",
